      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 3,
          "section": "3. Defining high-temperature oxidation resistance",
          "quote": "To illustrate the performance of a highly oxidation-resistant material, Fig. 5 shows a cross-section through the scale formed on NiCrAl-type alloy 214 (Haynes International) after 10,000 h at 1100 ◦C [16]. Due to a “third element effect” [17], the use of 17 at.% Cr in this alloy allows the formation of an alumina scale with a minimal (8.7%) Al addition. However, with this relatively low Al content, the alloy initially forms some Ni-rich oxide, remnants of which can be seen in the cross-section. This alloy is able to maintain an adherent alumina scale because it has a low sulfur content (sulfur being detrimental to scale adhesion [18]) and because of the addition of small amounts of Y (20 ppma) and Zr (120 ppma), so-called “reactive” elements [19].",
          "table": null,
          "figure": "Fig. 5",
          "chunk_id": "c02d53a859149a8a:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
